# depthmodel.cyr — RUNG 17's CORRECTNESS GATE: the depth shader's arithmetic at GPU REGISTER WIDTHS,
# byte-diffed against depthcore.cyr, which is the oracle.
#
# ⭐ THE POINT IS ATTRIBUTION, and rungs 11/13/15 earned it. Green here and red on iron ⇒ the fault is
# the EMISSION, not the algorithm. When rung 11's burn came back red, this same split reproduced the
# failing pixels bit-exactly on the host and convicted the shader in one pass instead of bisecting on
# iron.
#
# ============================================================================================
# WHAT THE MODEL ADDS OVER THE REFERENCE
# ============================================================================================
# 1. **THE AFFINE HOIST.** The reference evaluates three edge functions per pixel per triangle as
#    products of differences. The shader cannot afford that: it hoists each edge to `A*x + B*y + C`
#    and the depth numerator to `KX*x + KY*y + KC`, all computed ONCE PER TRIANGLE on the CPU, so the
#    per-pixel path is a few MACs and no cross products. Same doctrine as rung 13's record, whose
#    header says it outright: *everything constant per triangle belongs on the CPU.*
#    ⇒ Proving `hoisted == reference` is a real claim, not bookkeeping: the algebra that says the
#    edge function is affine in (x,y) is exactly where a sign or a term goes missing.
# 2. **32-BIT LANES, ON EVERY LANE, FROM THE RESIDUES THE RECORD ACTUALLY CARRIES.** Every accumulate
#    is masked to 32 bits and sign-restored, because `v_mul_lo_u32` / `v_add_u32` are 32-bit and the
#    compares that read them are `v_cmp_*_i32`.
#
# ============================================================================================
# ⛔⛔ THREE LANE-FIDELITY DEFECTS FIXED 2026-07-29 (plan bite B0). EACH ONE MADE THE MODEL PROVE
#     SOMETHING WEAKER THAN IT CLAIMED, AND EACH WAS GREEN.
# ============================================================================================
# (a) **`zn` WAS ACCUMULATED IN UNMASKED i64** while `w0/w1/w2` were masked and sign-restored beside
#     it. So the file's headline claim -- "at 32-bit lane widths" -- was true of the edge functions
#     and false of the depth numerator, which is the ONE value this rung is about.
# (b) **`KX/KY/KC` AND `area` WERE UNMASKED**, so the model computed from i64 coefficients the record
#     cannot hold. `KX = sum(A_i z_i)` reaches ~2^39 at legal inputs; the record stores **mod-2^32
#     residues** (plan 4.2 item 7). Computing from the true value instead of the residue is exact only
#     because mod-2^32 is a ring homomorphism -- which is a THEOREM THE MODEL SHOULD WITNESS, not
#     assume. `dm_ra/rb/rc` + `dm_kx32/ky32/kc32` now carry the residues and the lane path reads them.
# (c) **`zn` WAS EVALUATED ONLY INSIDE THE TRIANGLE.** The shader is PREDICATED, never branched (plan
#     4.4), so it evaluates `zn` on all 64 lanes of the tile including far outside. A bound derived
#     over inside pixels is a bound over a domain the shader does not run on. Measured by an attacker:
#     a triangle passing every proposed validator rule peaks at |zn| = 3.264e9 over its own bounding
#     box -- **1.52x past a 32-bit lane** -- while its inside peak is legal. A4 reported 2,430,400
#     (inside only) against a whole-frame peak; it now reports the whole frame, which is what the
#     validator's corner bound (plan 1.4 rule 18) has to be sized against.
#
# ⭐ The new gates are **A5** (every lane's 32-bit value equals its true value) and **A6** (the
# identity `w0 + w1 + w2 == area` AT LANE WIDTH, which the shader's derived `w2 = area - w0 - w1`
# depends on and which nothing had ever checked). M5/M6 prove both are connected.
# ⛔ M5 is the instructive one: it adds a MULTIPLE OF 2^32 to `KC`, so the residue -- and therefore
# every rendered pixel -- is **byte-identically unchanged**, and only A5 moves. That is a defect the
# reference diff is structurally incapable of seeing.
#
# ============================================================================================
# ⛔⛔ WHAT THIS FILE DOES **NOT** COVER, STATED FIRST SO THE GREEN IS NOT OVER-READ
# ============================================================================================
# The per-pixel divide `z = zn / area` is modelled here as an EXACT divide. The shader cannot do that
# — gfx9 has no integer divide — and will use the hoisted-reciprocal path (`recip32` + `tm_quot` +
# one exact correction) that rungs 9/13 already run on iron. That normalisation is NOT modelled here.
#
# ⚠ AND IT IS NOT A FORMALITY, because rung 15 established what the reciprocal can hide: nearest
# sampling consumed only the top 16 bits of its quotient, so 17/17 iron burns proved the divide only
# in the high half, and bilinear's 8 extra bits had never been compared by anything. **Depth compares
# the WHOLE quotient.** When the shader lands it needs texgate GATE 7's treatment — compare the full
# quotient word against an exact divide, not just the decision it feeds.
# ⇒ M2 below measures why that matters: it is the reason precision is load-bearing HERE and was not
# at rung 13.

include "lib/io.cyr"
include "tricore.cyr"      # ps / pn / nl
include "depthcore.cyr"

var DM_MASK32 = 4294967295;

# ---- mutations: each must break a gate --------------------------------------------------------
var dm_mut_dropc  = 0;   # M1 drop the hoist's constant term KC
var dm_mut_quant  = 0;   # M2 quantise z -- simulates a divide that loses low bits
var dm_mut_ulp    = 0;   # M2c z off by ONE -- the realistic divide error
var dm_mut_acc16  = 0;   # M3 truncate the accumulator to 16 bits
var dm_mut_swapk  = 0;   # M4 swap KX/KY
var dm_mut_kcbig  = 0;   # M5 add a MULTIPLE OF 2^32 to KC -- residue unchanged, true value not
var dm_mut_area1  = 0;   # M6 area off by one -- breaks the derived-w2 identity

var DM_QUANT = 4;        # M2's shift: z = (z >> 4) << 4

# dm_lane(v) — ONE 32-BIT LANE. Mask to 32 bits and restore the sign: exactly what a `v_mul_lo_u32`
# or `v_add_u32` result is when the `v_cmp_*_i32` that reads it interprets those 32 bits.
# ⚠ Forgetting the restore is the rung-11 unsigned-high-half defect in miniature.
fn dm_lane(v): i64 {
    var m = v & DM_MASK32;
    if (m >= 2147483648) { m = m - 4294967296; }
    return m;
}

# dm_hoist(t) — per-triangle CPU work: the three edge planes and the depth-numerator plane.
# Results land in globals, mirroring "one record per triangle, written by the CPU".
#
# ⭐ TWO PARALLEL SETS, DELIBERATELY. `dm_e*` / `dm_k*` are the TRUE i64 values -- the arithmetic
# truth, which nothing in the hardware ever holds. `dm_r*` / `dm_k*32` are the **mod-2^32 residues**,
# which is what a 32-bit record field actually carries and what the shader loads into an SGPR. The
# lane path reads ONLY the residues. Keeping both is what lets A5 assert they agree rather than
# assuming the homomorphism that makes the residues legitimate.
var dm_ea[3]; var dm_eb[3]; var dm_ec[3];      # edge i: A*x + B*y + C, TRUE i64
var dm_ra[3]; var dm_rb[3]; var dm_rc[3];      # the same three planes as mod-2^32 RESIDUES
var dm_kx = 0; var dm_ky = 0; var dm_kc = 0;   # depth numerator plane, TRUE i64
var dm_kx32 = 0; var dm_ky32 = 0; var dm_kc32 = 0;
var dm_area = 0;

fn dm_edge_plane(i, ax, ay, bx, by): i64 {
    # E(x,y) = (bx-ax)*(y-ay) - (by-ay)*(x-ax)
    #        = -(by-ay)*x + (bx-ax)*y + [ (by-ay)*ax - (bx-ax)*ay ]
    var a = 0 - (by - ay);
    var b = bx - ax;
    var c = (by - ay) * ax - (bx - ax) * ay;
    store64(&dm_ea + i * 8, a);
    store64(&dm_eb + i * 8, b);
    store64(&dm_ec + i * 8, c);
    store64(&dm_ra + i * 8, a & DM_MASK32);
    store64(&dm_rb + i * 8, b & DM_MASK32);
    store64(&dm_rc + i * 8, c & DM_MASK32);
    return 0;
}

fn dm_hoist(t): i64 {
    var ax = 2 * dc_get(t, 0); var ay = 2 * dc_get(t, 1);
    var bx = 2 * dc_get(t, 3); var by = 2 * dc_get(t, 4);
    var cx = 2 * dc_get(t, 6); var cy = 2 * dc_get(t, 7);
    # w0 opposes vertex a (edge b->c), w1 opposes b (c->a), w2 opposes c (a->b) -- the SAME
    # assignment depthcore uses. A permutation here would still sum to the area and would put the
    # wrong z on the wrong barycentric, which is the rung-15 weight-swap failure one level up.
    dm_edge_plane(0, bx, by, cx, cy);
    dm_edge_plane(1, cx, cy, ax, ay);
    dm_edge_plane(2, ax, ay, bx, by);
    var z0 = dc_get(t, 2); var z1 = dc_get(t, 5); var z2 = dc_get(t, 8);
    dm_kx = z0 * load64(&dm_ea + 0) + z1 * load64(&dm_ea + 8) + z2 * load64(&dm_ea + 16);
    dm_ky = z0 * load64(&dm_eb + 0) + z1 * load64(&dm_eb + 8) + z2 * load64(&dm_eb + 16);
    dm_kc = z0 * load64(&dm_ec + 0) + z1 * load64(&dm_ec + 8) + z2 * load64(&dm_ec + 16);
    if (dm_mut_swapk != 0) { var s = dm_kx; dm_kx = dm_ky; dm_ky = s; }
    if (dm_mut_dropc != 0) { dm_kc = 0; }
    # M5 -- a MULTIPLE of 2^32. The residue is bit-identical, so every rendered pixel is unchanged
    # and the reference diff cannot move. Only the lane-fidelity counter can see it.
    if (dm_mut_kcbig != 0) { dm_kc = dm_kc + 3 * 4294967296; }
    # ⭐ area is the SUM of the three edge planes and is constant in (x,y) -- the x and y terms
    # cancel identically. Computing it as the sum rather than as a fresh cross product is what makes
    # that cancellation observable rather than assumed.
    dm_area = load64(&dm_ec + 0) + load64(&dm_ec + 8) + load64(&dm_ec + 16);
    if (dm_mut_area1 != 0) { dm_area = dm_area + 1; }
    dm_kx32 = dm_kx & DM_MASK32;
    dm_ky32 = dm_ky & DM_MASK32;
    dm_kc32 = dm_kc & DM_MASK32;
    return 0;
}

var dm_cbuf[512];
var dm_zbuf[512];
var dm_ties = 0;
var dm_peak = 0;          # largest TRUE |zn| over EVERY LANE -- A4 checks it against a 32-bit lane
var dm_lane_bad = 0;      # lanes where the 32-bit residue path != the true i64 value  (A5)
var dm_sum_bad = 0;       # lanes where (w0 + w1 + w2) != area at lane width           (A6)

fn dm_render(w, h, zclear, bg): i64 {
    dm_ties = 0;
    dm_peak = 0;
    dm_lane_bad = 0;
    dm_sum_bad = 0;
    var py = 0;
    while (py < h) {
        var px = 0;
        while (px < w) {
            # ⚠ SCREEN coordinates -- dc_ox/dc_oy, exactly as dc_render does it. Missing this made the
            # model sample at x = 0 while the off-origin frame's vertices sat at x = 700; A7c caught it
            # on the first frame capable of seeing it, and the shipped corpus never could.
            var pxq = 2 * (dc_ox + px) + 1;
            var pyq = 2 * (dc_oy + py) + 1;
            var pz = zclear;
            var pc = bg;
            var t = 0;
            while (t < dc_n) {
                dm_hoist(t);
                # ---- ⭐ EVERY LANE EVALUATES, INSIDE OR NOT --------------------------------------
                # The shader is PREDICATED, never branched (plan 4.4): it computes w0/w1/w2 and zn on
                # all 64 lanes of the tile and masks only the UPDATE. So the model evaluates them
                # unconditionally too. Until 2026-07-29 `zn` was computed inside the `inside` test,
                # which made A4's peak a bound over a domain the shader does not run on.
                var w0t = load64(&dm_ea + 0)  * pxq + load64(&dm_eb + 0)  * pyq + load64(&dm_ec + 0);
                var w1t = load64(&dm_ea + 8)  * pxq + load64(&dm_eb + 8)  * pyq + load64(&dm_ec + 8);
                var w2t = load64(&dm_ea + 16) * pxq + load64(&dm_eb + 16) * pyq + load64(&dm_ec + 16);
                var znt = dm_kx * pxq + dm_ky * pyq + dm_kc;
                # ---- the SAME four numbers as the shader has them: from the mod-2^32 RESIDUES the
                # record carries, in 32-bit lanes, sign-restored for the signed compares.
                var w0 = dm_lane(load64(&dm_ra + 0)  * pxq + load64(&dm_rb + 0)  * pyq + load64(&dm_rc + 0));
                var w1 = dm_lane(load64(&dm_ra + 8)  * pxq + load64(&dm_rb + 8)  * pyq + load64(&dm_rc + 8));
                var w2 = dm_lane(load64(&dm_ra + 16) * pxq + load64(&dm_rb + 16) * pyq + load64(&dm_rc + 16));
                var zn = dm_lane(dm_kx32 * pxq + dm_ky32 * pyq + dm_kc32);
                var arl = dm_lane(dm_area);
                # ---- A4: the peak is of the TRUE value, over the WHOLE FRAME. That is the number the
                # validator's corner bound (plan 1.4 rule 18) must be sized against.
                var mag = znt;
                if (mag < 0) { mag = 0 - mag; }
                if (mag > dm_peak) { dm_peak = mag; }
                # ---- A5: the residue path agrees with the truth on this lane. Equality holds exactly
                # when the true value fits a signed 32-bit lane, because mod-2^32 is a ring
                # homomorphism -- so this counter IS the witness for that theorem, per lane.
                if (w0 != w0t) { dm_lane_bad = dm_lane_bad + 1; }
                if (w1 != w1t) { dm_lane_bad = dm_lane_bad + 1; }
                if (w2 != w2t) { dm_lane_bad = dm_lane_bad + 1; }
                if (zn != znt) { dm_lane_bad = dm_lane_bad + 1; }
                # ---- A6: the identity the shader's DERIVED w2 rests on -- `v_sub_u32 v7, s41, v5`
                # then `v_sub_u32 v7, v7, v6`. It is exact mod 2^32 only if the sum really is the area
                # at lane width, and nothing had ever checked that.
                if (dm_lane(w0 + w1 + w2) != arl) { dm_sum_bad = dm_sum_bad + 1; }
                var inside = 0;
                if (arl > 0) {
                    if (w0 >= 0) { if (w1 >= 0) { if (w2 >= 0) { inside = 1; } } }
                }
                if (arl < 0) {
                    if (w0 <= 0) { if (w1 <= 0) { if (w2 <= 0) { inside = 1; } } }
                }
                if (inside == 1) {
                    if (dm_mut_acc16 != 0) { zn = zn & 0xFFFF; }
                    var z = zn / arl;
                    # M2 -- a divide that loses low bits. See the gate for why this is the one that
                    # matters and why it did NOT matter at rung 13.
                    if (dm_mut_quant != 0) { z = (z >>> DM_QUANT) << DM_QUANT; }
                    if (dm_mut_ulp != 0) { if (t == 1) { z = z + 1; } }
                    if (z == pz) { dm_ties = dm_ties + 1; }
                    if (z < pz) { pz = z; pc = dc_get(t, 9); }
                }
                t = t + 1;
            }
            store32(&dm_cbuf + (py * w + px) * 4, pc & 0xFFFFFFFF);
            store32(&dm_zbuf + (py * w + px) * 4, pz & 0xFFFFFFFF);
            px = px + 1;
        }
        py = py + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# ---- the corpus ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⭐ FROM depthcore.cyr, NOT restated here. It WAS restated here until 2026-07-28, which meant the
# model could be validated against one pair of triangles while depthgate proved order-independence
# for another -- both green, neither reporting it. One definition; see dc_corpus's block.
fn dm_order(swap): i64 { return dc_corpus(swap); }

fn dm_vs_ref(): i64 {
    dc_render(DC_W, DC_H, DC_FAR, DC_BG);
    dm_render(DC_W, DC_H, DC_FAR, DC_BG);
    var d = 0;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < DC_W * DC_H) {
        var a = load32(&dc_cbuf + i * 4) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
        var b = load32(&dm_cbuf + i * 4) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
        var za = load32(&dc_zbuf + i * 4) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
        var zb = load32(&dm_zbuf + i * 4) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
        if (a != b) { d = d + 1; }
        if (za != zb) { d = d + 1; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return d;
}

var dm_snap[512];
fn dm_snapshot(): i64 {
    var i = 0;
    while (i < DC_W * DC_H) { store32(&dm_snap + i * 4, load32(&dm_cbuf + i * 4)); i = i + 1; }
    return 0;
}
fn dm_orderdiff(): i64 {
    dm_order(0); dm_render(DC_W, DC_H, DC_FAR, DC_BG); dm_snapshot();
    dm_order(1); dm_render(DC_W, DC_H, DC_FAR, DC_BG);
    var d = 0;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < DC_W * DC_H) {
        if ((load32(&dm_snap + i * 4) & 0xFFFFFFFF) != (load32(&dm_cbuf + i * 4) & 0xFFFFFFFF)) { d = d + 1; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return d;
}

# dm_frame_check(name) — run the CURRENT frame through the model and demand all three at once:
# byte-identity with the reference, zero lane overflows (A5), and the derived-w2 identity (A6).
# ⚠ Reads whatever `dc_*` corpus and `dc_ox/dc_oy` the caller set; it does not choose a frame itself.
fn dm_frame_check(name): i64 {
    var d = dm_vs_ref();
    ps(name);
    ps(" model==ref diff="); pn(d);
    ps("  lane-overflow="); pn(dm_lane_bad);
    ps("  sum-identity="); pn(dm_sum_bad);
    ps("  peak="); pn(dm_peak);
    if (d == 0) {
        if (dm_lane_bad == 0) {
            if (dm_sum_bad == 0) { ps("  PASS"); nl(); return 0; }
        }
    }
    ps("  FAIL"); nl();
    return 1;
}

fn dm_gate(name, d, want_red): i64 {
    ps(name);
    if (want_red == 0) {
        if (d == 0) { ps(" PASS"); nl(); return 0; }
        ps(" FAIL diff="); pn(d); nl(); return 1;
    }
    if (d != 0) { ps(" RED (correct -- the gate is connected), diff="); pn(d); nl(); return 0; }
    ps(" ** GATE NOT CONNECTED: the mutation changed nothing **"); nl(); return 1;
}

fn main(): i64 {
    var bad = 0;
    ps("depthmodel: rung 17 -- the depth shader's HOISTED arithmetic at GPU register widths"); nl(); nl();

    dm_order(0);
    bad = bad + dm_gate("A1 hoisted model == reference, colour AND z, order 0:", dm_vs_ref(), 0);
    dm_order(1);
    bad = bad + dm_gate("A2 hoisted model == reference, colour AND z, order 1:", dm_vs_ref(), 0);
    bad = bad + dm_gate("A3 the model is order-INDEPENDENT (the rung's oracle):", dm_orderdiff(), 0);

    # ---- A4/A5/A6 -- LANE FIDELITY, measured on one full frame -----------------------------------
    dm_order(0);
    dm_render(DC_W, DC_H, DC_FAR, DC_BG);

    # A4 -- the depth numerator must fit a SIGNED 32-bit lane, over the WHOLE FRAME. Reported,
    # because "it fits" is a claim about the CORPUS as much as the algebra, and a bigger frame moves
    # it. ⛔ Two things were wrong here until 2026-07-29: the peak was taken over INSIDE pixels only
    # (the shader is predicated and evaluates every lane), and the bound compared against 2^32-1
    # rather than 2^31-1. A value in [2^31, 2^32) sign-restores NEGATIVE, so it fails the compare it
    # feeds while passing an unsigned-width test -- A4 would have said PASS while A5 said FAIL.
    ps("A4 depth numerator peaks at "); pn(dm_peak); ps(" over EVERY lane (signed lane holds 2147483647): ");
    if (dm_peak <= 2147483647) { ps("PASS"); nl(); }
    if (dm_peak > 2147483647) { ps("FAIL"); nl(); bad = bad + 1; }

    ps("A5 every lane's mod-2^32 residue path == its true i64 value: ");
    if (dm_lane_bad == 0) { ps("PASS"); nl(); }
    if (dm_lane_bad != 0) { ps("FAIL, "); pn(dm_lane_bad); ps(" lane(s) overflowed"); nl(); bad = bad + 1; }

    ps("A6 w0 + w1 + w2 == area AT LANE WIDTH on every lane (the derived-w2 identity): ");
    if (dm_sum_bad == 0) { ps("PASS"); nl(); }
    if (dm_sum_bad != 0) { ps("FAIL, "); pn(dm_sum_bad); ps(" lane(s)"); nl(); bad = bad + 1; }

    # ---- A7 -- THE OTHER THREE FRAMES (plan bite B2) ---------------------------------------------
    # ⛔ A5 above runs on the shipped corpus, where |KC| = 115,200 and every 32-bit residue IS its own
    # true value. That is the EXACT NULL SET of the error A5 is written to catch -- the same shape as
    # rung 15's half-texel, whose gates probed integer coordinates a pixel-centre rasteriser can never
    # emit. A7c is where the residue path finally carries a value the record cannot hold.
    nl();
    dc_corpus_precision(0);
    bad = bad + dm_frame_check("A7a PRECISION  ");
    dc_corpus_quad(0);
    bad = bad + dm_frame_check("A7b QUAD       ");
    dc_ox = DC_OFF_X;
    dc_corpus_offorigin(0);
    bad = bad + dm_frame_check("A7c OFF-ORIGIN ");
    dm_hoist(0);
    var kcmag = dm_kc;
    if (kcmag < 0) { kcmag = 0 - kcmag; }
    # ⭐ A8 -- THE FRAME MUST ACTUALLY OVERFLOW, or A5 is a check with nothing to catch. This is the
    # gate that stops the off-origin frame degrading back into a null set the way the plan's original
    # x=700 parameters silently did (|KC| = 58,124,800, 26 bits, inside an i32).
    ps("A8  |KC| = "); pn(kcmag); ps(" vs the signed 32-bit field it must NOT fit in (2147483647): ");
    if (kcmag > 2147483647) { ps("PASS -- the residue path is load-bearing here"); nl(); }
    if (kcmag <= 2147483647) {
        ps("FAIL -- KC fits an i32, so A5 has nothing to catch on any frame in this tree"); nl();
        bad = bad + 1;
    }
    ps("    the record carries the mod-2^32 residue "); pn(dm_kc32);
    ps("; A5 proves it reconstructs zn exactly at every lane, and A4's peak shows why"); nl();
    dc_ox = 0;
    nl();

    # ---- mutations -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    dm_order(0);
    dm_mut_dropc = 1;  bad = bad + dm_gate("M1 hoist constant term KC dropped:", dm_vs_ref(), 1);  dm_mut_dropc = 0;
    dm_mut_swapk = 1;  bad = bad + dm_gate("M4 KX/KY swapped:", dm_vs_ref(), 1);                   dm_mut_swapk = 0;
    dm_mut_acc16 = 1;  bad = bad + dm_gate("M3 numerator truncated to 16 bits:", dm_vs_ref(), 1);  dm_mut_acc16 = 0;

    # ---- M5: THE ONE THE REFERENCE DIFF CANNOT SEE ----------------------------------------------
    # Adding 3*2^32 to KC leaves the mod-2^32 residue bit-identical, so the shader's data, and
    # therefore every rendered pixel, is unchanged. Only A5 can move. This is the shape of the
    # off-origin failure the plan's OFF-ORIGIN frame will exercise for real (|KC| = 243e9 at x=700
    # against 115,200 on this corpus), demonstrated here at zero geometry cost.
    dm_mut_kcbig = 1;
    dm_order(0);
    var m5ref  = dm_vs_ref();
    var m5lane = dm_lane_bad;
    dm_mut_kcbig = 0;
    ps("M5 KC + 3*2^32 (RESIDUE UNCHANGED): reference diff="); pn(m5ref);
    ps("  lane violations="); pn(m5lane); nl();
    if (m5lane == 0) {
        ps("   ** A5 NOT CONNECTED: a true value 3*2^32 outside the lane moved nothing **"); nl();
        bad = bad + 1;
    }
    if (m5ref != 0) {
        ps("   ** MUTATION IS NOT WHAT IT CLAIMS: the residue changed, so this is not a pure"); nl();
        ps("      lane-fidelity probe and A5's connection is unproven **"); nl();
        bad = bad + 1;
    }
    if (m5lane > 0) {
        if (m5ref == 0) {
            ps("   ^ RED on A5, INVISIBLE to the reference diff -- the exact defect class A5 exists for"); nl();
        }
    }

    # ---- M6: the derived-w2 identity ------------------------------------------------------------
    dm_mut_area1 = 1;
    dm_order(0);
    dm_render(DC_W, DC_H, DC_FAR, DC_BG);
    var m6sum = dm_sum_bad;
    dm_mut_area1 = 0;
    ps("M6 area off by one: "); pn(m6sum); ps(" lane(s) break w0+w1+w2 == area: ");
    if (m6sum > 0) { ps("RED (correct -- A6 is connected)"); nl(); }
    if (m6sum == 0) { ps("** A6 NOT CONNECTED **"); nl(); bad = bad + 1; }

    nl();
    # ---- ⭐⭐ M2: THE ONE THAT MATTERS, AND THE REASON IT DID NOT MATTER AT RUNG 13 ---------------
    # Quantising z to 16 levels is what a divide that loses its low bits would do. At rung 13 a
    # one-ULP quotient error was INVISIBLE -- nearest sampling truncated it away, which texgate's
    # gate 6 measured directly (186 texels moved, against 3151 quotients that actually changed).
    # ⛔ DEPTH COMPARES THE WHOLE QUOTIENT. A lost low bit turns two distinct z values into a TIE,
    # and a tie is decided by SUBMISSION ORDER -- so precision loss does not merely perturb a pixel,
    # it destroys the order-independence property that IS the rung's iron oracle.
    dm_mut_quant = 1;
    dm_order(0); dm_render(DC_W, DC_H, DC_FAR, DC_BG);
    var qties = dm_ties;
    var qorder = dm_orderdiff();
    dm_mut_quant = 0;
    ps("M2a quantising z creates TIES where there were none: ");
    if (qties > 0) { ps("RED (correct), "); pn(qties); ps(" tie px"); nl(); }
    if (qties == 0) { ps("** NOT CONNECTED: no ties appeared **"); nl(); bad = bad + 1; }
    ps("M2b and those ties BREAK order-independence: ");
    if (qorder > 0) { ps("RED (correct), "); pn(qorder); ps(" px differ between orders"); nl(); }
    if (qorder == 0) { ps("** NOT CONNECTED: order-independence survived **"); nl(); bad = bad + 1; }

    # ---- M2c: IS A **ONE-ULP** ERROR VISIBLE? Measure, do not assume. ---------------------------
    dm_mut_ulp = 1;
    dm_order(0); dm_render(DC_W, DC_H, DC_FAR, DC_BG);
    var uties = dm_ties;
    var uorder = dm_orderdiff();
    dm_order(0);
    var uref = dm_vs_ref();
    dm_mut_ulp = 0;
    # ⛔⛔ MEASURED, AND IT REFRAMES M2a/M2b. Quantising z by 16 levels breaks order-independence
    # loudly (3 px). A **one-ULP** error -- the realistic shape of a divide that is short by one --
    # moves order-independence by **1 pixel out of 507**, while the DIRECT comparison against the
    # reference moves by **217**. Two orders of magnitude apart, on the same defect.
    # ⇒ **ORDER-INDEPENDENCE IS A NEARLY-BLIND DETECTOR OF DIVIDE ERROR.** It is the rung's stated
    # iron oracle and it is the WRONG instrument for this failure mode: one pixel is exactly the size
    # that reads as noise, and a slightly different error shape would move zero.
    # ⚠ This does NOT retract M2a/M2b -- precision really is load-bearing. It bounds them: the
    # property is load-bearing, and the ORACLE built on it is not sensitive enough to police it.
    # ⇒ THE DIVIDE NEEDS ITS OWN GATE, comparing the WHOLE quotient word against an exact divide over
    # the ABI's admissible range -- texgate GATE 7's treatment, for exactly the reason rung 15 needed
    # it. Order-independence cannot stand in for it, and on iron z is not even readable.
    ps("M2c ONE-ULP z error on one triangle: ties="); pn(uties);
    ps("  orderdiff="); pn(uorder); ps("  vs-reference="); pn(uref); nl();
    ps("    ^ order-independence moves "); pn(uorder); ps(" px; direct z/colour comparison moves ");
    pn(uref); ps(" -- the ORACLE is ~200x less sensitive than the reference diff."); nl();

    nl();
    if (bad == 0) {
        ps("depthmodel: PASS -- the hoisted, 32-bit-lane model is byte-identical to the reference in"); nl();
        ps("            colour AND z, is order-independent, and every mutation breaks it."); nl();
        ps("⭐ M2a/M2b: z PRECISION IS LOAD-BEARING -- quantising z creates TIES, and a tie is"); nl();
        ps("  resolved by SUBMISSION ORDER, so precision loss attacks the ORACLE itself."); nl();
        ps("⛔ M2c BOUNDS THAT CLAIM, AND IT IS THE MORE USEFUL HALF: a ONE-ULP error moves"); nl();
        ps("  order-independence by 1 px out of 507 while moving the direct reference diff by 217."); nl();
        ps("  ⇒ ORDER-INDEPENDENCE IS A NEARLY-BLIND DETECTOR OF DIVIDE ERROR -- one pixel is the"); nl();
        ps("  size that reads as noise, and a different error shape would move zero. The rung's"); nl();
        ps("  stated iron oracle is the WRONG instrument for this failure mode."); nl();
        ps("  ⇒ THE DIVIDE NEEDS ITS OWN GATE (texgate GATE 7's treatment: compare the WHOLE"); nl();
        ps("  quotient word against an exact divide). On iron z is not readable at all, so this"); nl();
        ps("  gate cannot be deferred to the burn."); nl();
        return 95;
    }
    ps("depthmodel: FAIL -- "); pn(bad); ps(" gate(s) wrong; do NOT write the shader yet."); nl();
    return 90;
}

fn _entry(): i64 { var r = main(); syscall(SYS_EXIT, r); return 0; }
_entry();
